This section converts a directional offshore wind resource and a turbine layout into wake-adjusted farm power states and annual energy production. These outputs determine gross wind-farm yield and provide the operating states used by the electricity and hydrogen conversion calculations.
The calculation is performed in dependency order:

fit a directional wind-speed distribution;
place the selected number of turbines inside the modelled farm area;
assign turbine power and thrust-coefficient curves;
calculate wake-affected power for each wind-speed and direction state;
probability-weight the states to obtain annual energy production; and
pass the wake-aware power table to downstream conversion calculations.

This section converts a directional offshore wind resource and a turbine layout into wake-adjusted farm power states and annual energy production. These outputs determine gross wind-farm yield and provide the operating states used by the electricity and hydrogen conversion calculations.
The calculation is performed in dependency order:

fit a directional wind-speed distribution;
place the selected number of turbines inside the modelled farm area;
assign turbine power and thrust-coefficient curves;
calculate wake-affected power for each wind-speed and direction state;
probability-weight the states to obtain annual energy production; and
pass the wake-aware power table to downstream conversion calculations.

Cost Boundary
The base calculation includes:

mobilisation and demobilisation;
port loading and sea fastening;
vessel travel between port and the wind farm;
inter-turbine vessel movements;
normal monopile and transition-piece installation; and
normal turbine erection.

It excludes:

turbine and foundation supply;
marshalling-port construction and rent;
feeder-vessel logistics;
scour protection;
boulder and unexploded-ordnance removal;
relief drilling and refusal remediation;
project-specific underwater-noise mitigation;
extraordinary standby and major repairs; and
electrical commissioning outside normal installation work.

These excluded activities are not assigned a zero cost. They remain outside the base calculation until separately parameterised.

Treatment of Mass Uncertainty
The turbine and foundation masses are screening estimates rather than supplier-certified transport masses.
Material uncertainties include:

unresolved turbine electrical-component masses;
a length-based rather than load-based tower estimate;
missing tower-section masses;
missing transition-piece mass; and
simplified monopile scaling without site-specific structural or geotechnical design.

These uncertainties matter because sets per load is an integer. A small mass change near a payload threshold can change the number of vessel trips.
The model therefore uses upstream mass sensitivity results where available:

calculate the payload limit at the lower mass;
calculate it again at the upper mass;
adopt the result when both limits give the same sets per load; and
report both logistics cases when the result crosses an integer boundary.

No separate arbitrary mass safety factor is added.

Why the Model Stops Here
The model does not calculate deck packing, blade-rack arrangement, tower-section lifts, crane radius or detailed stability.
Those calculations would require additional assumptions for:

component dimensions;
tower segmentation;
rack geometry;
grillage and access space;
crane outreach;
rigging mass;
stability limits; and
project-specific lift arrangements.

The turbine-system model does not currently provide enough information to constrain those assumptions. Adding them would increase model complexity and apparent precision without a corresponding increase in accuracy.
Mass is therefore used as a constraint on a sourced logistics parameter, not as a replacement for a vessel loading plan. It captures differences between turbine designs without requiring the installation model to know why their masses differ.
Mass also does not change site occupation, vessel speed or day rate continuously. There is no adopted hours-per-tonne or EUR-per-tonne installation relationship.

Calibration Priorities
Additional evidence should be collected in this order:

effective turbine site occupation in days/turbine;
coherent vessel rate, payload, crane and sets-per-load data for the same named vessel or spread;
weather-inclusive foundation and turbine campaign productivity;
transition-piece mass or directly evidenced foundation sets per load;
contractor-defined mobilisation and complete-spread boundaries; and
independent project schedules for validation.

The present model is intended for transparent screening comparisons. It is not a marine-operations simulation or contractor quotation.

Derived Quantities
The deterministic workflow derives, where the owning component is parameterized:

installed wind rated capacity;
turbine coordinates and farm area;
wake-adjusted production;
cable, pipeline and platform inventories;
equipment counts and discrete link or pipeline counts;
conversion losses and auxiliary demand;
pre-availability and delivered annual energy;
initial CAPEX, replacement, decommissioning and annual OPEX; and
annualized carrier-specific cost.

Required Checks Before Aggregation
Before costs are added:

source currency, price year and scope are known;
adopted values are normalized to EUR2025;
component, installation and project boundaries do not overlap;
required unresolved fields return not parameterized;
replacement is counted only under the documented rule;
decommissioning is applied once to total offshore installation CAPEX; and
annual OPEX is not embedded in CAPEX or another component term.

Scope of the Length Result
The calculated length includes the horizontal rungs and the two horizontal headers. It excludes:

the export pipeline from the manifold to shore;
turbine risers, local tie-ins and short spool pieces;
the export manifold equipment itself;
route deviations around seabed constraints, crossings and exclusion zones;
installation slack and contingency; and
parallel pipe added where segment capacity is insufficient.

No 20% routing allowance is included in the current hydrogen base case. The source applies that allowance to the analytical AC-cable layout, not to the hydrogen ladder. A separate routing factor may be applied before detailed routing:
\[
L_\mathrm{routed}
=
L_\mathrm{infield}
\left(1+f_\mathrm{route}\right).
\]
Do not apply this factor to a GIS-derived route that already includes deviations and slack.

Cable Construction
A typical fixed-bottom array cable contains:

three copper or aluminium phase conductors;
conductor screens and cross-linked polyethylene (XLPE) insulation;
metallic screens and water-blocking layers;
fibre-optic elements for communication and monitoring;
fillers, bedding and one or more armour layers; and
an outer serving that protects the cable during handling and installation.

Cable cross-section normally increases toward the offshore substation because each successive segment carries the power of more downstream turbines. A screening model can use one usable string rating, but an optimised design uses at least two or three conductor sizes.

Land Use, Planning and Environment
Onshore conversion moves impacts from sea to land. The main siting considerations are:

proximity to a sufficiently strong high-voltage node;
space for the permanent station and temporary construction compound;
cable corridors, joint bays and road access for heavy transformers;
audible noise from transformers, reactors and cooling;
visual effects and building height;
electromagnetic-field and safety clearances;
flood, drainage and soil conditions;
ecology, archaeology and community acceptance; and
heat rejection and auxiliary electricity supply.

Long underground routes can dominate civil works even when the converter site is close to the coast. Conversely, placing the converter near landfall may require a separate 380 kV AC route to the grid. Route and station location should therefore be optimised together.

Capacity Model
Pipeline capacity is calculated explicitly from the pressure drop available between the pipeline inlet and outlet. The implemented calculation follows the gas-flow method used in the Transition Accelerator hydrogen pipeline brief (Transition Accelerator 2023) and the Colebrook friction-factor treatment in Perry’s Chemical Engineers’ Handbook (Perry’s Chemical Engineers’ Handbook, n.d.). The implementation uses the Fanning-friction convention from Perry’s equations rather than the Darcy-Weisbach convention.
The cross-sectional area is:
\[
A = \frac{\pi D^2}{4}
\]
The available pressure drop is:
\[
\Delta p
=
p_\mathrm{in}
-
p_\mathrm{out}
\]
The code evaluates hydrogen density and viscosity at an average pipeline pressure:
\[
p_\mathrm{avg}
=
\frac{2}{3}
\frac{
p_\mathrm{in}^3 - p_\mathrm{out}^3
}{
p_\mathrm{in}^2 - p_\mathrm{out}^2
}
\]
The average density and viscosity are then obtained from CoolProp:
\[
\rho_\mathrm{avg}
=
\rho(T,p_\mathrm{avg})
,
\qquad
\mu_\mathrm{avg}
=
\mu(T,p_\mathrm{avg})
\]
where \(T\) is 293 K (pipeline-temperature).
The implementation then evaluates the combined term \(\mathrm{Re}\sqrt{f_F}\):
\[
\mathrm{Re}\sqrt{f_F}
=
\frac{D^{3/2}}{\mu_\mathrm{avg}}
\sqrt{
\frac{
\Delta p \rho_\mathrm{avg}
}{
2L
}
}
\]
where \(f_F\) is the Fanning friction factor. The Colebrook relation is applied as:
\[
\frac{1}{\sqrt{f_F}}
=
-4
\log_{10}
\left(
\frac{\epsilon}{3.7D}
+
\frac{1.256}{\mathrm{Re}\sqrt{f_F}}
\right)
\]
where \(\epsilon\) is the pipe roughness, 0.00000015 m (pipeline-roughness), and 3.7 and 1.256 are the Colebrook constants shown in the equation.
The resulting gas velocity is:
\[
v
=
\sqrt{
\frac{
D \Delta p
}{
2 \rho_\mathrm{avg} L
}
}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{f_F}}
\]
The mass-flow capacity is:
\[
\dot{m}_\mathrm{max}
=
\rho_\mathrm{avg}
A
v
\cdot
3600
\]
where \(\dot{m}_\mathrm{max}\) is expressed in kg/h.
The transport capacity is expressed as an HHV-equivalent power:
\[
P_{\mathrm{H_2},\mathrm{HHV}}
=
\dot{m}_\mathrm{max}
\cdot
\frac{
\mathrm{HHV}_\mathrm{H_2}
}{
1000
}
\]
where \(\mathrm{HHV}_\mathrm{H_2}=39.4 \ \mathrm{kWh/kg}\) and \(P_{\mathrm{H_2},\mathrm{HHV}}\) is in MW when \(\dot{m}_\mathrm{max}\) is in kg/h.
Figure 1 shows the resulting capacity curves over an illustrative diameter and inlet-pressure range, for the reference-case route of 100 km and outlet pressure of 67.01 bar(a).






Figure 1: Hydrogen export-pipeline capacity as a function of inlet pressure and diameter.

Dependence on Pressure and Diameter
Pressure enters the calculation through both the pressure drop, \(\Delta p\), and the pressure-dependent hydrogen properties evaluated at \(p_\mathrm{avg}\):
\[
\Delta p = p_\mathrm{in} - p_\mathrm{out}
\]
This means that increasing inlet pressure raises capacity nonlinearly, but with diminishing benefit when expressed against the compressor penalty. Higher pressure can allow one pipeline to carry more hydrogen, but the same pressure increase also raises compressor electricity consumption and compressor CAPEX. Pipeline pressure is therefore optimised jointly with the compressor model rather than selected independently.
Diameter has an even stronger effect on capacity. Area scales with \(D^2\), while the velocity equation adds a further \(\sqrt{D}\) term before friction effects. Ignoring the secondary effect of friction factor, capacity therefore scales approximately as:
\[
\dot{m}_\mathrm{max}
\propto
D^{2.5}
\sqrt{
\Delta p \rho_\mathrm{avg}
}
\]
This explains why larger diameters tend to have lower cost per unit of transport capacity. The economic optimum often prefers the largest feasible composite diameter. However, export pipelines are discrete assets. If one large pipeline is oversized for the required peak flow, reducing pressure may lower compressor cost while retaining enough capacity. Conversely, a higher pressure can be justified if it avoids an additional parallel pipeline.
The export pipeline count is:
\[
n_\mathrm{pipe}
=
\left\lceil
\frac{
\dot{m}_{\mathrm{H_2},\mathrm{peak}}
}{
\dot{m}_\mathrm{max}(D,p_\mathrm{in},p_\mathrm{out},L)
}
\right\rceil
\]
where \(\dot{m}_{\mathrm{H_2},\mathrm{peak}}\) is the peak hydrogen flow that must be exported. This discrete ceiling is important: small changes in pressure, diameter, stack sizing, or wind-farm peak production can change the required number of pipelines.

Efficiency and Auxiliary Demand
Efficiencies and auxiliary loads are applied in physical component order before annual availability. The owner of each component defines:

input and output energy or mass;
efficiency equation and operating basis;
auxiliary demand and where it is withdrawn;
applicable time step; and
whether any outage or derating is already embedded.

The result is architecture-specific pre-availability annual energy, \(E_{\mathrm{preavailability},a}\).

Availability Combination
For architecture \(a\):
\[
E_{\mathrm{delivered},a}
=
E_{\mathrm{preavailability},a}
\prod_{s\in S_a} A_s,
\]
where \(S_a\) contains only non-overlapping annual subsystem factors.
The rules are:

each outage cause appears once;
serial non-overlapping subsystem factors may be multiplied;
the component owner supplies an already-combined factor for parallel units or redundancy;
a factor is omitted when the effect is embedded in the upstream energy calculation;
an unresolved required factor returns not parameterized for complete delivered energy; and
100% is permitted only as an explicit zero-outage scenario assumption, not as an evidence-backed reliability result.

Calculation Logic
For each case, the model:

validates the user inputs and fixed parameters;
constructs the wind-farm layout and gross production;
selects the electricity, centralised-hydrogen or decentralised-hydrogen component chain;
calculates physical inventories, component sizing, losses and auxiliary demand;
returns an explicit infeasible or not-parameterized status when a required constraint or input is unresolved;
applies annual subsystem availability once at architecture level;
aggregates non-overlapping capital and operating costs; and
reports the carrier-specific energy and cost outputs.

Electricity and hydrogen are delivered at different onshore interfaces. Their costs may be shown side by side, but they are not equivalent end-use service prices.

Calculation Sequence
The calculation is performed in dependency order:

calculate rated rotor speed and torque;
calculate blade and hub-system masses;
calculate drivetrain, nacelle, electrical, and tower masses;
select the components present in the chosen drivetrain and electrical architecture;
aggregate rotor, nacelle, RNA, tower, and complete-turbine masses;
calculate raw component costs; and
calibrate the raw WISDEM cost to a stated offshore-turbine cost reference.

Cost Calibration
The raw WISDEM costs are not used as the absolute turbine price. Separate RNA and tower calibration factors preserve WISDEM’s component scaling while matching a stated NREL offshore reference:
\[
k_\mathrm{RNA}
=\frac{C_\mathrm{RNA,ref}^{\mathrm{NREL}}}
{C_\mathrm{RNA,ref}^{\mathrm{WISDEM}}},
\qquad
k_\mathrm{tower}
=\frac{C_\mathrm{tower,ref}^{\mathrm{NREL}}}
{C_\mathrm{tower,ref}^{\mathrm{WISDEM}}}.
\]
The calibrated turbine supply cost is:
\[
C_\mathrm{turbine}
=k_\mathrm{RNA}C_\mathrm{RNA,raw}
+k_\mathrm{tower}C_\mathrm{tower,raw}.
\]
The current calibration candidate is NREL’s 2022 Cost of Wind Energy Review. Its offshore reference is a 12000 kW turbine with a 216 m rotor and 137 m hub height. It reports turbine CAPEX of USD 1,700/kW, separated into 1462 USD/kW for the RNA and 238 USD/kW for the tower; installation is a separate cost category (Stehly et al. 2023). These values define a reference point, not a linear EUR/kW turbine model.
Currency conversion and escalation are applied after calibration using the common EUR2025 financial basis. The source currency, source year, index, and exchange-rate convention must accompany every reported result.
